{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git diff HEAD~1 HEAD --stat; git rev-parse HEAD; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md'","aggregated_output":" docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md | 856 ++++++++++++++++++-----------\n 1 file changed, 529 insertions(+), 327 deletions(-)\ne0c843c976a5f1177563194a11a9b97b943e698f\n     1\t# UCF transported state as tuples: the row carrier (body version 3)\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: **Draft, proposed, not landed.** Written 2026-10-10 from the owner\n     4\tdiscussion recorded in\n     5\t`public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog`\n     6\tPart Three; revised the same day after the codex Architect review\n     7\t`collab/1791500000000-architect-ucf-transport-tuples-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n     8\t(thirteen findings, all addressed below, and six answers adopted in §10).\n     9\tSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md), to UCF §7 in\n    10\t[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md),\n    11\tand to the version-2 amendment\n    12\t[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md),\n    13\twhose body is the fact list this carrier must reproduce. Follows the row\n    14\tconventions of [`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md).\n    15\tNothing here is implemented. Body versions 0, 1 and 2 keep their grammar,\n    16\treaders and bytes; this document proposes a fourth body version whose\n    17\tcarrier is rows, and says what it must preserve.\n    18\t\n    19\t## 1. Stance\n    20\t\n    21\tThree owner rulings fix the shape of this document.\n    22\t\n    23\t1. *Cross-VM safepoints are semantic-VM safepoints.* The Universal AST is\n    24\t   the canonical code; the instruction vector is its deterministic\n    25\t   lowering; a safepoint is `(segment-address, pc)` into that vector\n    26\t   (UCF §7.3.2, §7.4.1). This carrier changes nothing about code\n    27\t   identity, safepoints, or execution profiles.\n    28\t2. *The transported state is a tuple set.* Sharing and the literal/marker\n    29\t   distinction stop being special cases: a reference is a row id in a\n    30\t   typed position, sharing is Merkle, and a program's data is wrapped\n    31\t   where it can be confused with a reference. What does not collapse is\n    32\t   the per-entity decision between content and identity, which this\n    33\t   carrier makes a property of the row kind and then commits to the root\n    34\t   through a census.\n    35\t3. *Tuples are the transport form only.* They exist between a lift and a\n    36\t   lower. At rest they are rows like any other and queryable by the\n    37\t   ordinary interpreter. Nothing executes them; the destination lowers\n    38\t   them into its native representation.\n    39\t\n    40\tThis proposal chooses positional rows: a row kind fixes a slot list, one\n    41\ttable serves encoder and decoder, and a row's Merkle id commits to its\n    42\tchildren inline, as code rows already do. EAV is expressive enough and\n    43\tnothing in the invariants rules it out; positional rows are chosen for\n    44\tcompactness, closedness, and consistency with the existing row\n    45\tconventions, not because EAV was rejected by review.\n    46\t\n    47\t## 2. Positioning: body version 3, same profiles\n    48\t\n    49\tBody versions 0 and 1 (UCF §7.2.1) and 2 (the amendment) are nested\n    50\tcanonical-CBOR maps. **This carrier is body version 3.** The outer\n    51\t`:yin.k/version` gate of §7.2.1 and amendment §8 step 1 runs first and\n    52\tunchanged: a reader that does not speak 3 refuses with\n    53\t`:yin.k/profile-mismatch` carrying the version found and the set it\n    54\tspeaks, before any row is read. A version-3 reader keeps versions 0, 1\n    55\tand 2 exactly as their contracts state and never upgrades a body across\n    56\tversions.\n    57\t\n    58\tThe execution profile is untouched. `:yin.k/contract` carries exactly the\n    59\tamendment §1 profile map `{:yin.k/engine e :yin.code/contract c\n    60\t:yin.k/version 1}`, from the same registry, under the same whole-tree\n    61\tgate (one profile per task and install tree). Code stamps `\"v3\"`, `\"b2\"`,\n    62\t`\"r2\"` and the inner version 1 do not move: this is a change of carrier,\n    63\tnot of instruction, scheduler, or value semantics, exactly the\n    64\tdistinction the amendment draws between its versions and executable\n    65\tcode.\n    66\t\n    67\tThis document tables the **semantic** profile's registers, frames and\n    68\tclosures in full. The stack, register and walker profiles use the same\n    69\tcarrier with their own `R`, `F`, layout and free-env rows, to be tabled\n    70\tin a follow-up against amendment §5.2–5.4; the carrier admits them by\n    71\tprofile but this draft does not yet specify their rows. Like version 2,\n    72\tversion 3 transports native suspended state within a profile and does not\n    73\ttranslate between kernels. The foreign-engine case (an engine that\n    74\timplements canonical semantic-VM execution in its own layout, UCF §7.4.2)\n    75\tis the semantic profile lowered through that engine's value profile, and\n    76\tvalue profiles are a separate document (§10.6).\n    77\t\n    78\t## 3. Rows, id spaces, and the census\n    79\t\n    80\tEvery row is `[id kind & slots]`; the stored payload is the body\n    81\t`[kind & slots]`; the id is an address envelope outside the hashed bytes,\n    82\tas for code rows (code-as-tuples §2.1).\n    83\t\n    84\t**Content rows** have `id = (dao.jing/segment-key body)`, the canonical\n    85\tCBOR of the body under the established digest. Equal bodies are one row.\n    86\tThe id is Merkle: it commits to every row id in the body.\n    87\t\n    88\t**Identity rows** are cells (UCF §7.5.3). A cell has a task-local id `C`\n    89\tin the form `:yin.k/c-<n>`, minted by the lift under §6's canonical\n    90\tnumbering, never content-addressed, and scoped to the task whose root\n    91\tcarries it: a cell id means nothing outside its root, in a pool or a\n    92\tquery, and two tasks' `:yin.k/c-1` are unrelated.\n    93\t\n    94\t**The census.** A cell's *contents* (its stream marker and kept position)\n    95\tare not reachable through its id, so a content row that references a\n    96\tcell commits to the cell's identity but not to what the cell holds. The\n    97\troot therefore carries a **cell census**, a content row\n    98\t\n    99\t```clojure\n   100\t[X :cells  C₁ B₁  C₂ B₂  …]          ; sorted by n; Bᵢ is a cref\n   101\t[Bᵢ :cell-body  stream-cref  position]\n   102\t```\n   103\t\n   104\tthat maps every cell id to the content row of its body. The census is a\n   105\tslot of the root, so the root id commits to every cell's contents. A\n   106\tchange to any cell's position or stream changes `Bᵢ`, the census, and\n   107\tthe root. Identity references preserve aliasing; the census\n   108\tauthenticates contents. The validation rules (§8) require the census to\n   109\tlist exactly the cells the task reaches: none missing, none extra, per\n   110\troot and per install child.\n   111\t\n   112\t**Acyclicity.** Two edge families exist and are checked separately:\n   113\t\n   114\t- *Structural value edges*: a cref in a slot, a cell iref in a value, a\n   115\t  census entry. Merkle construction cannot build a cref cycle bottom-up,\n   116\t  and a `:cell-body` references only a stream (content) and a position\n   117\t  (data), so no cell closes a cycle. The receiver still checks\n   118\t  acyclicity over this family explicitly (§8 step 4), because a receiver\n   119\t  validates, it does not trust construction.\n   120\t- *Context lookup edges*: a `:parked-ref` row names a parked id `P`\n   121\t  resolved through the task's parked table; a closure's `store-of` names\n   122\t  a manifest address `M` resolved through the module stores; an\n   123\t  `:install` pending names a module whose entry holds a child body.\n   124\t  These may close cycles and that is legal: a module store that holds a\n   125\t  closure whose `store-of` is that same module is the ordinary shape of\n   126\t  a linked module, and self- or mutually-referencing parked records\n   127\t  resolve through the explicit `P` table exactly as amendment §6 states.\n   128\t  A guest value that would need a cycle through *structural* edges is\n   129\t  refused at lift as `:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:cyclic` naming the\n   130\t  path, unchanged from UCF §7.5.3.\n   131\t\n   132\t## 4. The value union\n   133\t\n   134\tEvery position that holds a guest value holds one **encoded value** `V`,\n   135\ta two-element vector whose first element is a fixed tag:\n   136\t\n   137\t| `V` | Meaning |\n   138\t|---|---|\n   139\t| `[:lit d]` | a literal: `d` is in the portable literal domain below, carried by Jing as data |\n   140\t| `[:row B]` | a reference to the content row `B` (a closure, a collection row, a frame value, a primitive, a stream marker) |\n   141\t| `[:cell C]` | a reference to the identity cell `C` |\n   142\t\n   143\tThe union is disjoint by the tag, not by the shape or spelling of `d`: a\n   144\tliteral keyword that happens to equal a row address is `[:lit k]` and\n   145\tcan never be read as a reference; a literal vector that looks like a\n   146\t`[:row …]` pair is `[:lit [...]]`. No decision depends on content, which\n   147\tis the UCF §7.5.1 property restated for rows.\n   148\t\n   149\t**The portable literal domain** is UCF §7.5.1's scalar and collection\n   150\tarms, closed under nesting: nil, booleans, exact integers of any width\n   151\t(Jing major types 0/1, tags 2/3), float64 carriers, strings, keywords,\n   152\tsymbols, and vectors, lists, sets and maps *all of whose elements, keys\n   153\tand values are in the domain*. Scalar and collection carrier classes are\n   154\tretained, never coerced (an integral float stays a float). Byte strings\n   155\tare not admitted by this carrier and refuse as `:yin.k/non-portable`\n   156\tkind `:byte-string`. Metadata: a literal travels with whatever metadata\n   157\tJing's canonical encoding preserves for it, hashed as Jing hashes it; a\n   158\tcollection that must become a row (because it reaches a reference) has\n   159\tno metadata slot, and one that carries metadata refuses as\n   160\t`:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:metadata` naming the path. A guest map is\n   161\talways wrapped, `[:lit m]` or a `:map` row, and is open data inside the\n   162\twrapper; structural rows are closed.\n   163\t\n   164\tA collection whose elements include a reference is not a literal; it\n   165\tbecomes a row (§5.1) and the position holds `[:row B]`. This is the only\n   166\tstructural decision the encoder makes and it is decided by one question,\n   167\t*does this collection reach a reference?*, answered by walking it.\n   168\t\n   169\tPositions that hold structural data (addresses, pcs, symbols as binding\n   170\tnames, reasons, phases, ids) are typed by the row table and are not `V`;\n   171\ttheir kinds are fixed per slot, as for code rows.\n   172\t\n   173\t## 5. The row table\n   174\t\n   175\tKinds are closed. An unknown kind, a wrong arity, a slot whose content is\n   176\tnot of the tabled kind, or a `V` outside §4's union is `:yin.k/undecodable`\n   177\tnaming the row and slot. \"Sorted\" means ordered by Jing canonical-byte\n   178\torder of the sort key, and the sort key never contains a cell reference\n   179\t(§6). Ordered slots are part of the schema because they determine the\n   180\tMerkle id.\n   181\t\n   182\t`N` is an exact CBOR integer in `[0, 2^52−1]` (amendment §2). `A` is a\n   183\tcode address, `M` a manifest address, `P` a parked id, `C` a cell id,\n   184\t`S` a symbol.\n   185\t\n   186\t### 5.1 Values (content rows)\n   187\t\n   188\t| Kind | Slots | Decodes to |\n   189\t|---|---|---|\n   190\t| `:vec` | `V …` in order | vector |\n   191\t| `:list` | `V …` in order | list |\n   192\t| `:set` | `V …` sorted by the element's cell-free skeleton (§6) | set |\n   193\t| `:map` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …`, each `kᵢ` a `V`, sorted by the key's cell-free skeleton | map |\n   194\t| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁ S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | `{S V}` |\n   195\t| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | E: the named environment plus, when `store-of` is an `M`, `engine/store-of-key` (amendment §2) |\n   196\t| `:closure` | `segment-A  entry-pc-N  params  bindings-cref  store-of` | semantic closure (amendment §6 semantic arm); `params` is a plain vector of symbols checked against the addressed lambda |\n   197\t| `:prim` | `name-S` | the named primitive after the profile check (UCF §7.5.2); the profile lives in requires |\n   198\t| `:stream` | `identity  descriptor` | a stream marker; both plain, authenticated at lift and re-sealed at lower |\n   199\t| `:reified` | `regs-cref` | `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/frame :yin.k/registers R}`: a captured continuation, no wait |\n   200\t| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/frame :yin.k/parked-id P}`: resolves in the task's parked table; never duplicates `R` |\n   201\t\n   202\t`:reified` and `:parked-ref` keep the two outer discriminants amendment\n   203\t§6 requires and lower differently: a reified value becomes a\n   204\treceiver-owned native continuation from `R`; a parked reference\n   205\treconstructs the engine's parked value for `P`.\n   206\t\n   207\t### 5.2 Semantic registers, waits, frames (content rows)\n   208\t\n   209\t| Kind | Slots |\n   210\t|---|---|\n   211\t| `:regs` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  stack-cref  k-cref` |\n   212\t| `:stack` | `V …` bottom first |\n   213\t| `:k` | `kframe-cref …` bottom to top |\n   214\t| `:kframe` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  stack-base-N` |\n   215\t| `:wait` | `regs-cref  pending-cref` |\n   216\t\n   217\t`:regs` is amendment §5.1's `R` for the semantic profile, and nothing\n   218\telse: no reason, no pending. A wait is a `:wait` row pairing registers\n   219\twith a pending; a reified value and a parked record reference `:regs`\n   220\tdirectly. `:kframe` is exactly the semantic return frame `{:type :return\n   221\tsegment pc env stack-base}`; the semantic profile has no other frame\n   222\ttype on the wire (walker effect frames belong to the walker profile's\n   223\town table). Resume pc and return pc must lie inside their segment, the\n   224\treturn pc immediately after a non-tail call, stack bases nondecreasing\n   225\tfrom outermost to innermost and each at most the enclosing stack length\n   226\t(amendment §5.1), all checked in §8.\n   227\t\n   228\t### 5.3 Pending (content rows)\n   229\t\n   230\tOne kind, `:pending`, whose first slot is the reason and whose remaining\n   231\tslots are fixed per reason. The reasons are UCF §7.4.3's closed union as\n   232\tthe amendment r5 required-keys list fixes them; `:park` and\n   233\t`:call-effect` are not reasons and refuse as `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n   234\t\n   235\t| reason | slots after the reason |\n   236\t|---|---|\n   237\t| `:next` | `cell-C` |\n   238\t| `:put` | `stream-cref  value-V  op-id` |\n   239\t| `:ffi` | `cell-C  call-id  op` (op may be nil) |\n   240\t| `:ffi-request` | `call-id  request-envelope-V  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  response-cell-C  op-id` |\n   241\t| `:link-request` | `link-id  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C  envelope-V  op-id` |\n   242\t| `:link-response` | `link-id  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C` |\n   243\t| `:install` | `name-S` |\n   244\t\n   245\t`op-id` is `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` as plain data or nil,\n   246\tpresent exactly when the write was first attempted through a fenced\n   247\twriter, carried verbatim, never minted or renumbered by lift or lower,\n   248\tand subject to every rule of §7.4.3 r5 (below the root's\n   249\t`:yin.k/next-op-seq`, unique across the task and its children, absent\n   250\twhen the body has no origin). `request-envelope` and `envelope` are the\n   251\tretained requests, retried verbatim. `call-id` and `link-id` are plain.\n   252\tAn `:install` pending is never sufficient alone: the root's installs\n   253\tslot must hold the entry for `name`, else lift refuses\n   254\t`:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:incomplete-install` and a body carrying it\n   255\tis `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n   256\t\n   257\t### 5.4 Task context (content rows)\n   258\t\n   259\t| Kind | Slots |\n   260\t|---|---|\n   261\t| `:store` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …` sorted by the key's cell-free skeleton; the isolated slice of UCF §7.6.2 |\n   262\t| `:module-store` | `manifest-M  store-cref` |\n   263\t| `:parked-table` | `P₁ regs-cref₁ P₂ regs-cref₂ …` sorted by P |\n   264\t| `:install` | `name-S  phase  parent  response-V  child-cref` |\n   265\t| `:cells` | the census, §3 |\n   266\t| `:cell-body` | `stream-cref  position` (position in the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` portable cursor domain) |\n   267\t\n   268\tResource entries never appear in `:store`; streams and cells lower into\n   269\tthe receiver's private resource table beside the program store (linker\n   270\tM4, `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3). `:module-store` rows are the per-module\n   271\tsnapshots of §7.6.2's M4 paragraph. The parked table holds exactly the\n   272\treachable records. An `:install` row carries the complete entry of\n   273\t§7.4.3 r5: phase `:running` or `:parked`, parent link id (nil where the\n   274\tcontract permits), the verified response verbatim as a `V`, and the\n   275\tchild as a cref to a whole `:task` row in the child role.\n   276\t\n   277\t### 5.5 The root\n   278\t\n   279\t```clojure\n   280\t[T :task\n   281\t   version          ; 3                       (gated first, §8)\n   282\t   contract         ; the profile map, plain  (amendment §1)\n   283\t   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n   284\t   role             ; :root | :child\n   285\t   id-counter       ; N                       (UCF §7.6.3)\n   286\t   store-cref\n   287\t   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n   288\t   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n   289\t   cells-cref       ; :cells census\n   290\t   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map, unchanged\n   291\t   code             ; plain: {A code-payload …}, each payload verified to hash to its key (§7.2.1)\n   292\t   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n   293\t   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, IN WAIT ORDER   (:blocked, :parked)\n   294\t   parked-id        ; P or nil                              (:parked only)\n   295\t   result           ; V or nil                              (:halted only)\n   296\t   custody]         ; plain, see below\n   297\t```\n   298\t\n   299\tKind rules are §7.2.1's: `:blocked` has nonempty frames and neither\n   300\tparked-id nor result; `:parked` has frames (possibly empty), a parked-id\n   301\tnaming a key of the parked table, no result; `:halted` has a result and\n   302\tneither frames nor parked-id. Frames restore in carried order and are\n   303\tnever collapsed to one; a task with an empty ready queue may hold several\n   304\tordered waits.\n   305\t\n   306\t`custody` is the version-1 header carried as plain data, under the same\n   307\trules (§7.2.1, amendment §3): on a `:root` of kind `:blocked` or\n   308\t`:parked` under `:yin.k/exclusive`, exactly `{:yin.k/policy\n   309\t:yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/origin :yin.k/next-op-seq}`\n   310\twith origin absent on a first export; a fork root carries `{:yin.k/policy\n   311\t:yin.k/fork}` and nothing else; a `:halted` root carries origin only; a\n   312\t`:child` carries nil and is validated with its root's occurrence, origin\n   313\tand counter passed down. No body carries an epoch.\n   314\t\n   315\tThe root is a content row and **its id is the transported state's\n   316\taddress**, the `B` of UCF §7.3.4 for this carrier. Occurrence and origin\n   317\tare inside it, as the governing rule keeps them (§10.1). The code map is\n   318\tplain data in the root rather than a row kind: each instruction vector\n   319\tkeeps its own address `A` computed exactly as UCF §7.3.2 computes it, and\n   320\tthe carrier verifies, never recomputes, that address.\n   321\t\n   322\t## 6. Canonical cell numbering\n   323\t\n   324\tCell ids must be a function of the state, or two lifts of one state give\n   325\ttwo root ids. The numbering is computed **before** any row is hashed and\n   326\twithout reference to any hash, in one pass:\n   327\t\n   328\t1. **Traversal order.** Walk the task in this fixed order, recursing\n   329\t   into every reachable structure: frames in wait order, and within a\n   330\t   wait its registers then its pending; within registers, env (bindings\n   331\t   by sorted symbol, then store-of's module store if not yet walked),\n   332\t   stack bottom-up, k bottom-to-top with each frame's env; the parked\n   333\t   table by sorted `P`; the store by sorted key skeleton; module stores\n   334\t   by sorted `M`; the result; install entries by sorted module name,\n   335\t   each child recursively as its own task with its own numbering.\n   336\t   Within a value: vectors and lists in order; sets and maps by the\n   337\t   element's or key's **cell-free skeleton**, the value's canonical bytes\n   338\t   with every cell reference replaced by the constant `[:cell nil]`;\n   339\t   closures by segment, entry, then bindings; pendings in slot order.\n   340\t2. **Assignment.** The first time a cell is reached it receives the next\n   341\t   `n`; a cell reached again keeps its number. Distinct cells at equal\n   342\t   streams and positions are distinct and receive distinct numbers.\n   343\t3. **Ties.** Two elements of a set, or two keys of a map, with equal\n   344\t   skeletons are ordered by the canonical bytes of their referenced\n   345\t   cells' *bodies* (stream marker, then position), walked in the\n   346\t   element's own traversal order. Two elements that are equal in\n   347\t   skeleton and in every referenced cell body are symmetric: either\n   348\t   assignment produces the same row set and the same root id, so the\n   349\t   choice is immaterial and the encoder takes them in encounter order.\n   350\t\n   351\tBecause skeletons omit cell numbers and ties are broken by cell bodies,\n   352\tthe ordering never depends on a number the pass has yet to assign. Rows\n   353\tare then built and hashed with the numbers fixed. The receiver\n   354\t**recomputes the numbering from the decoded rows and refuses**\n   355\t(`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind `:noncanonical-cells`) if the carried ids\n   356\tdiffer from the recomputed ones; contiguity alone is not canonicality.\n   357\t\n   358\tUnder this rule two lifts of one state are byte-identical. Two lifts of\n   359\tdifferent states sharing a closure over the same cell may number it\n   360\tdifferently and so not share that closure's row across lifts; that\n   361\tcross-lift dedup is knowingly given up for identity-bearing values and\n   362\tnever affected rows below a cell. On lower, each `C` becomes one fresh\n   363\tprivate resource entry seeded at its carried position; every `[:cell C]`\n   364\tmaps to that entry; two references to one cell share it and one\n   365\treference per cell keeps its own (UCF §7.5.3).\n   366\t\n   367\t## 7. Equality and the content address\n   368\t\n   369\tThe transported state is a DAG rooted at the `:task` row. Its address is\n   370\tthe root id, which commits to every reachable content row, to every cell\n   371\tid through the references, and to every cell's contents through the\n   372\tcensus. Two transported states are equal iff their root ids are equal,\n   373\tand under §6 that holds iff they were lifted from states equal up to the\n   374\trenaming of cell ids that §6 makes canonical. No physical enumeration of\n   375\tthe row set needs an order, because the root commits to everything and\n   376\tthe pack, if one is addressed, has its own canonical encoding (§10.2).\n   377\tJing supplies canonical bytes and the digest of each body; this document\n   378\tsupplies the order within each body and the numbering of cells. Jing is\n   379\tasked to sort nothing it does not already sort.\n   380\t\n   381\tOccurrence and origin are inside the hash, as in versions 1 and 2 where\n   382\tthe body address is the hash of the whole body. Snapshot variants of one\n   383\toccurrence may therefore have distinct addresses while remaining one\n   384\toccurrence, exactly as UCF §7.3.4 intends.\n   385\t\n   386\t## 8. Validation on lower\n   387\t\n   388\tValidation is pure until the last step and checks the whole tree before\n   389\tone attachment. The order follows amendment §8 and is binding:\n   390\t\n   391\t1. **Gate.** Decode canonical bytes retaining numeric classes. The root\n   392\t   row's kind is `:task` and its version is the exact integer 3, else\n   393\t   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` (version found, supported set). Traverse\n   394\t   install children in canonical module-name order; gate every child's\n   395\t   version, then every profile map against the registry, then require\n   396\t   all versions 3 and all profiles equal to the root's. A malformed\n   397\t   install container is `:yin.k/undecodable`; nothing claims to have\n   398\t   gated a non-task.\n   399\t2. **Structure.** Every row's kind is tabled with the right arity; every\n   400\t   structural slot holds its tabled kind; every `V` is in §4's union and\n   401\t   every literal in the portable domain; no two rows share an id; exactly\n   402\t   one row has role `:root`; kind rules of §5.5 hold; the custody slot\n   403\t   matches the role, kind and policy.\n   404\t3. **Hashes.** Every content row's id equals the Jing address of its\n   405\t   body; every code payload hashes to its key `A` and is well formed\n   406\t   under the stamp; the requested body address, if one was given, equals\n   407\t   the root id. Mismatch is `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the row or\n   408\t   code key.\n   409\t4. **Graph.** Every cref names a present row of a kind admissible in that\n   410\t   slot; the structural edge family is acyclic; every row is reachable\n   411\t   from the root (an unreachable row is `:yin.k/undecodable`); every\n   412\t   `[:cell C]` names a census entry and every census entry is reached,\n   413\t   per task and per child; every `:parked-ref` and `parked-id` resolves\n   414\t   in the parked table; every `store-of` resolves to a carried module\n   415\t   store; every `:install` pending has its entry and every entry's child\n   416\t   is a `:child` task.\n   417\t5. **Canonical numbering.** Recompute §6 and compare (kind\n   418\t   `:noncanonical-cells` on mismatch).\n   419\t6. **Code and frames.** Each resume pc is a static safepoint whose kinds\n   420\t   admit the wait's reason (`:yin.k/reason-mismatch`); a parked record's\n   421\t   pc is an explicit-park safepoint; return pcs follow a non-tail call;\n   422\t   stack bases are bounded and nondecreasing; closure params agree with\n   423\t   the addressed lambda; integer slots are in range.\n   424\t7. **Pending and operations.** Reason-specific slot rules; every op-id\n   425\t   below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent without origin;\n   426\t   link and FFI correlation fields present.\n   427\t8. **Dependency closure.** `requires` evaluated per UCF §7.6.5: missing\n   428\t   segments, primitives, modules, streams, cursor profiles are\n   429\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied`; a body with cells claims\n   430\t   `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; module-store coverage is complete for every\n   431\t   `store-of` reached.\n   432\t9. **Custody.** For an exclusive root, the occurrence, baseline and\n   433\t   operation inspector and admission checks of §7.7.8; an authority\n   434\t   never adds missing header keys.\n   435\t10. **Composition.** Protection, resource, primitive and grant inputs of\n   436\t    the receiving composition.\n   437\t11. **Restore.** Reconstruct isolated code spaces, fresh values, the\n   438\t    isolated store, module-store instances (first link wins), one\n   439\t    private resource entry per cell, the parked table, children first,\n   440\t    then waits in order; adopt `max(local, carried)` for the id counter;\n   441\t    publish the machine only after every child succeeds; run, poll,\n   442\t    mint, append, close or initialize nothing during lower.\n   443\t\n   444\tDiagnostics use the established families and no new top-level outcome:\n   445\t`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, `:yin.k/undecodable` with `:yin.k/path` as\n   446\t`[row-id slot-index …]`, `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied`,\n   447\tand the lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` kinds, including the three this\n   448\tcarrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string`, `:metadata`,\n   449\t`:noncanonical-cells`.\n   450\t\n   451\t## 9. Lift, lower, and the laws\n   452\t\n   453\t**Lift** (reference machine): refuse unless quiescent; number cells (§6);\n   454\twalk waits, parked records, the slice, module stores, the result and\n   455\teach install child, emitting rows and the census; refuse by path as UCF\n   456\t§7.5.4 and §7.2.1 specify. The nested carrier's value table\n   457\t(`:yin.k/values`) has no counterpart, sharing being Merkle; its cell\n   458\ttable becomes the census.\n   459\t\n   460\t**Lower**: §8 in order, then restore.\n   461\t\n   462\tThe laws, with the domains the Architect's finding 12 requires:\n   463\t\n   464\t- **Canonical round trip.** For every valid, canonical (§6-numbered)\n   465\t  version-3 state `S` within a supported profile,\n   466\t  `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id, under a reconstruction context that\n   467\t  preserves every carried canonical field: the id counter is compared as\n   468\t  carried, not after `max`; occurrence, origin, cell bodies and op-ids\n   469\t  are carried verbatim; resource remapping is undone by the lift's own\n   470\t  numbering.\n   471\t- **Native observational equivalence.** `lower(lift(m))` need not equal\n   472\t  `m` as native state (fresh ids, seals, private keys); under equivalent\n   473\t  external inputs it must preserve the next observable step, the wait\n   474\t  set and its order, the result, and the effect trace.\n   475\t- **Cross-engine.** Running the corpus to each safepoint on an engine\n   476\t  that implements canonical semantic-VM execution yields, under\n   477\t  controlled composition identities (occurrence, origin, resource\n   478\t  descriptors fixed by the harness), the same version-3 row set the\n   479\t  reference machine yields.\n   480\t- **Legacy correspondence.** A version-0/1/2 body normalizes into\n   481\t  version 3 with semantic preservation (same lowered machine under\n   482\t  native equivalence); exact mutual inversion is required only between\n   483\t  version 3 and its one canonical nested projection (§10.3), never\n   484\t  against historical nested encodings with arbitrary sharing thresholds\n   485\t  and cell names.\n   486\t\n   487\tThe UCF §7.11 harness gains one assertion per law. During transition the\n   488\tStage D/E corpus, re-encoded through the canonical projection, is the\n   489\toracle for the first law.\n   490\t\n   491\t## 10. Questions the review answered, and what remains\n   492\t\n   493\tAnswers 1–6 are the codex Architect's (round 1); where the Architect\n   494\tmarked a point as an owner decision it is labelled so.\n   495\t\n   496\t1. **Occurrence inside the hash.** Kept inside, following the governing\n   497\t   rule and binding custody context to the published checkpoint. An\n   498\t   occurrence-independent execution-state address is an owner-level\n   499\t   protocol decision; if ever pursued it must sit beside, not replace, a\n   500\t   fully authenticated checkpoint address, and it does not substitute\n   501\t   for the census.\n   502\t2. **Packing.** Individual immutable content rows plus a self-contained\n   503\t   transport pack. When a pack is addressed it gets its own address and\n   504\t   canonical encoding, distinct from the semantic root id; cell ids\n   505\t   remain root-scoped inside it. Same answer as code-as-tuples §2.1\n   506\t   should receive; the loader interface is resolved explicitly, not by\n   507\t   layout.\n   508\t3. **The nested grammar.** Versions 0, 1 and 2 remain compatibility\n   509\t   inputs. One canonical nested projection of version 3 is kept for\n   510\t   single-message channels and must stay exactly mutually inverse with\n   511\t   the rows. Rows are the carrier authority once specified. Retiring old\n   512\t   readers is an owner version-support decision.\n   513\t4. **Contract stamp.** The profile map and code contracts do not move; a\n   514\t   carrier change is a new body version with its own gate. Any change to\n   515\t   instruction or scheduler semantics would separately revise the\n   516\t   execution contract and is not proposed here.\n   517\t5. **Reified and parked.** Sharing immutable `:regs` rows is acceptable;\n   518\t   the two keep distinct outer kinds (§5.1) and distinct lowering. A\n   519\t   reified capture has no pending; a parked reference resolves in task\n   520\t   context.\n   521\t6. **Foreign-engine value profiles.** Required before any implementation\n   522\t   brief claims heterogeneous execution conformance, and not drafted\n   523\t   here: admitted values, numeric behaviour, key equality, metadata,\n   524\t   resource and profile requirements, refusal sets, and safepoint\n   525\t   reconstruction obligations. Slot losslessness alone does not\n   526\t   establish execution parity, and version 3, like version 2, does not\n   527\t   translate between kernels.\n   528\t\n   529\tStill open after this revision:\n   530\t\n   531\t7. The stack, register and walker profile row tables (§2).\n   532\t8. Whether a version-3 reader should accept version-2 bodies for lower\n   533\t   only, or whether normalization (law 4) is a separate tool.\n   534\t9. The exact cell-free skeleton encoding (§6): `[:cell nil]` as the\n   535\t   placeholder is a proposal; the Architect should confirm it cannot\n   536\t   collide with a literal.\n   537\t\n   538\t## 11. What this does not change\n   539\t\n   540\tCode identity, the contract stamp and profile registry, safepoints,\n   541\tpending-wait semantics and the r5 required keys, install-child rules,\n   542\tthe dependency closure and satisfaction check, the isolated store, the\n   543\tprivate resource table, module-store instancing, scheduler state, the\n   544\tcustody header and admission rules, fencing and operation ids, the\n   545\toutcome families, and the existing refusal kinds (three lift kinds are\n   546\tadded, §8). The carrier is a new body version for the same facts. The\n   547\tArchitect's round-1 statement is the acceptance bar: tuple transport\n   548\tviolates none of the six invariants once polymorphic references and\n   549\tcontext edges are specified and validated, and §4, §3 and §8 are where\n   550\tthey are.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '168,217p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; sed -n '471,542p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; sed -n '632,710p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; rg -n 'metadata|decimal|ratio|plain-data|requires|response' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc | head -45\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '168,217p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; sed -n '471,542p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; sed -n '632,710p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; rg -n 'metadata|decimal|ratio|plain-data|requires|response' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc | head -45\"","aggregated_output":"## 3. Custody arms, roles and admission\n\nFork/exclusive is derived from presence of the custody arm, not a new\nmode flag and not body version alone. Let H be the set of keys\n`{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/origin\n:yin.k/next-op-seq}`. No top-level epoch or enrolled set is permitted.\n\n| Structural role | Legal intersection with H | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Root blocked/parked, fork | empty | fork; no custody admission |\n| Root blocked/parked, exclusive first | policy, occurrence, arbitration, next-op-seq | new custody subject |\n| Root blocked/parked, exclusive successor | previous four plus origin | successor custody subject |\n| Root halted, fork | empty | ordinary result |\n| Root halted, exclusive result | origin only | result of the origin lease; no new subject |\n| Install child, any kind | empty | inherits its root's custody role |\n\nEvery other intersection is `:yin.k/undecodable`. Thus origin alone\nidentifies an exclusive halted result; it is not a partial runnable\nheader. Empty header maps do not travel. The policy, when required, is\nexactly `:yin.k/exclusive`. Occurrences are the existing canonical UUID\nstrings. Arbitration is exactly the existing identity/descriptor pair.\nOrigin is exactly predecessor occurrence, lease and emitter; no new keys.\nThe successor differs from its predecessor. First next-op-seq is zero;\nsuccessor next-op-seq is the exact carried counter. Exhaustion, inherited\noperation scope and baseline equality remain UCF 7.7.8 rules.\n\nA fork tree forbids carried custody operation IDs. An exclusive tree\nchecks every retained operation, including descendants, against the\nroot's origin and baseline. Children have no independently granted epoch,\nlease, counter or enrollment. Code profile and custody are independent:\nall four profiles admit both root modes.\n\nFor an exclusive runnable root, lower requires the D10 inputs:\ncheckpoint address, protection declaration, authenticated binding and\nreplay-prefix evidence, lease/holder, arbitration transaction identity,\nexact epoch, and current live tenure. Enforce all seven landed grant\nchecks and both directions of enrollment/protection agreement before\nattachment. Missing grant yields awaiting-grant and no runnable VM;\ncontradictory binding/tenure yields not-holder; unavailable evidence or\nprotection yields unsatisfied, as in D10. The driver still proves the\naddress is an admitted variant and rechecks tenure before execution/IO.\n\nAfter admission the root has the existing custody map and running gate;\nchildren have the gate only. An exclusive halt restores ended, without\ncustody or a grant. Fork lower is an explicit composition choice: an\nexclusive-only receiver refuses a fork with profile-mismatch, even if it\nsupports that execution profile. Administrative export-recovery lower is\nseparate and always remains exporting (section 10).\n\n## 4. Code identity and image layout\n## 6. Values, closures and dependency census\n\nUse the disjoint UCF 7.5 value grammar. In particular, every guest map\nis a `:yin.k/literal` marker with encoded key/value entries. Structural\nregister maps, AST rows and pending maps are not guest literal maps.\nScalars/collections retain the canonical Jing carrier class. Resource\nreferences are authenticated at lift and reminted under the receiver's\nowner at lower. No source capability secret travels.\n\nClosure markers use the existing kernel module seams, now validated\nunder the enclosing profile:\n\n| Profile | Marker fields beyond `:yin.k/tag :yin.k/closure` |\n| --- | --- |\n| semantic | binding `:named`, format `:yin.semantic/code`, segment A, entry local PC, params, env bindings, optional store-of |\n| stack | binding `:positional`, format `:yin.debruijn.code`, segment H, entry image-relative PC, arity, frames F, optional store-of |\n| register | binding `:positional`, format `:yin.debruijn.register`, segment R, entry image-relative PC, arity, frames F, optional store-of |\n| walker | binding `:named`, format `:yin.ast/code`, segment lambda-row A, entry nil, params, env bindings, optional store-of |\n\nAll field names above carry the existing `:yin.k/` namespace. Named\nclosure env is the existing `{S V}` bindings map, not E; its store-of is\nthe sibling marker key. Positional entry points are image-relative even\nthough live register PCs are absolute. Params/arity must agree with the\naddressed lambda/closure instruction, not merely with the wire claim.\nUse the kernel's lower-closure validation; never relabel another\nprofile's closure. Every frame value is recursively encoded.\n\nVersion 2 makes the parent document's reified/parked frame-value arms\nexplicit; it does not change legacy readers which currently refuse some\nof these values. A reified value is exactly:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/frame :yin.k/registers R}\n```\n\nA parked reference is exactly:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/frame :yin.k/parked-id P}\n```\n\nThe latter refers to the same task's parked table; do not duplicate R\ninside it. Authenticate source-owned continuation values, validate R in\ncaptured-value context (section 7), and create a receiver-owned native\ncontinuation. For a parked reference, reconstruct the engine's parked\nvalue associated with P. A parked body derives its active value from\nparked-id, not an extra result. Self/mutually referenced parked records\nresolve through the explicit P table; other cycles without a declared\nidentity fail as non-canonicalizable. No new general object-identity or\nheap serialization scheme is authorized. Existing unsupported mutable\nheap-cell payloads remain non-portable.\n\nThe fixed-point census starts from waits, pending values/envelopes,\nreachable parked records, guest store slice, free-env when present,\nmodule-store associations, halted result and each install child. Traverse\nboth keys and values, every frame arm, closure captures and nested frame\nvalues. Iterate module stores/code until no dependency is added. Resolve\nRule-R names from the source's own profile interpretation, never from the\nreceiver's guest state. Missing source dependencies refuse; a known\nportable primitive unavailable in the receiver is unsatisfied.\n\nCells are task-local: encode aliasing as one C; distinct source cells\nremain distinct even if positions equal. Assign IDs deterministically\nbefore order-sensitive encoding, using the established canonical\nordering pass, not comparator side effects. Canonical encoded key order\norders maps and sets; it must not reorder waits, layouts, positional\nframes, register pairs or continuation chains. Every declared cell must\nbe reached and every reached cell declared. Apply the same rule per\nchild. Pin roots needed by outstanding FFI/stream obligations according\nto the existing heap/resource rules; serialization is not reclamation.\n\n## 7. Eligibility and safepoint validation\n## 8. Validation order and diagnostics\n\nValidation is pure until step 6 below. Check the entire tree before\nperforming even one resource attachment.\n\n1. Decode the canonical codec while retaining numeric classes. Check\n   handoff tag and outer body version. An unreadable object is\n   undecodable; an absent, non-integer or unsupported body version is\n   profile-mismatch. A v0-only/v1-only reader refuses 2 here.\n2. Traverse structurally identifiable install children in canonical\n   module-name order. Check all body versions, then all profile maps\n   against the supported registry. Only after these gates check that\n   all versions are 2 and all profiles equal the root's. A malformed\n   install container that cannot be traversed is undecodable; do not\n   claim to have profile-checked a non-body. Every known child is gated\n   before hashing code, attachment, proposal or restoration.\n3. Validate closed body/custody/register/value/pending/layout shapes,\n   canonical bytes and the requested body address, native code hashes,\n   code validators, frame eligibility, marker/code agreement, complete\n   dependency closure and nested install roles. Unsupported cursor or\n   primitive requirements yield unsatisfied. No host execution probes\n   are used to validate code or values.\n4. For an exclusive root, run the existing occurrence/baseline/operation\n   inspector and admission checks. An authority rejects fork bodies\n   as custody checkpoints; it never adds missing policy/header keys.\n   Completion accepts exclusive halted results only under their origin.\n5. Check receiving composition protection/resource/primitive requirements\n   and grant inputs. Unavailable evidence is not an empty enrollment or\n   empty replay prefix. No runnable VM is returned on refusal.\n6. Reconstruct isolated code spaces, fresh values, stores, resources and\n   children. Apply the permitted gate/custody state and publish the\n   machine only after every child succeeds. Do not run, poll, mint,\n   append, close, initialize modules or consume a delivery during lower.\n\nProfile mismatch includes `:yin.k/path`, the found version/contract and\nthe supported set. Structural errors use `:yin.k/undecodable` with the\nexact path; bad content identities use `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` and identify\nbody or code key. Unsupported execution profile is never reported as a\nhash error first. Lift refusals use existing non-portable kinds for\nunaddressed code, noncanonical values and inconsistent state, with path\nand explanatory data; introduce no new top-level outcome algebra.\n\nCounter exhaustion retains the existing distinctions: incoming exhausted\nbaseline is inspectable but cannot allocate another operation; export\nrefuses an exhausted next-op-seq according to the existing lift rule.\nBody versions and code PCs are not epochs. No new authority dedup keys,\nlease facts or DaoStream outcome keys are added.\n\n## 9. Lowering and isolation contract\n\nChoose the receiver kernel from the validated profile and composition,\nnot from a guest module registry. A supplied receiver of another profile\nis profile-mismatch, never a request to compile or translate. Clear guest\nstore, module stores, parked records, installs, wait/ready queues, closes,\nissued state and kernel code layout before rebuilding. Retain only\ncomposition-owned host module entries, named primitive/profile registry\nand administrative resource/secret factories. No initialization reruns.\n\nRestore waits in wire order; rebuild per-task issue stamps/counters as\nin D10 rather than importing unrelated receiver history. Preserve carried\noperation IDs and the root's exact next-op-seq; D11 assigns only genuinely\nnew operations. Replay starts from the existing zero-to-frontier prefix\ncontract; the snapshot does not invent an input record. FFI and link\napply paths must preserve request-to-response phase transitions.\n\nFor stack/register, the current layout owns code; captured prefix\nlayouts reconstruct continuation payloads, not new running code spaces.\nClosure image-relative locations resolve against current offsets. For\nwalker, build the row-node index before materializing closures or Kw\nframes. Use the receiver's authentic constructors; source owner objects\nand receiver-local guest bindings cannot supply dependencies.\n\nRestoration itself delivers no wait result. A test's controlled later\nengine apply followed by normal scheduling is what makes execution\ncontinue. An exclusive machine is runnable only after grant admission;\na fork is explicitly permitted by composition; a result has no work to\nschedule. Installs carry their existing parent/phase and scheduler role.\n\n## 10. Export recovery, integration and implementation boundary\n34:   restoration passes.  A failed lower installs nothing partially\n41:   with composition-supplied grant evidence and protection declarations;\n48:   whole child -- the child's own handoff body, phase and response --\n88:   before the tag grammar and before any address or restoration check --\n261:   never depends on a host's map or set iteration; as given under\n504:                     :link-request :link-response :install}})\n512:   nil: a response reader's `eval-call` carrying its call id, or a\n534:   alone: a `:next` wait with a call id is a sent FFI response reader;\n557:    :link-response :link-response\n593:   or a `:link-request` entry whose response cursor is not installed.\n620:;; Protection (UCF 7.4.3, 7.7.8): the operation id against the enrolled set\n626:   the header's enrolled set must carry an operation id -- one attempted\n662:  \"The emitter's call-out cursor cell -- the response route a retained\n678:   reaches, and the response route a retained call held is a cell like\n703:    (let [route (if (and (:response-cursor entry)\n704:                         (:response-stream entry)\n706:                  {:id (:response-cursor entry)\n707:                   :stream-id (:response-stream entry)}\n721:           :yin.k/response (marker-for! vm serve! found\n723:           :yin.k/response-cell (cell-for! vm serve! found\n726:    (:link-request :link-response)\n734:               :yin.k/response (marker-for! vm serve! found\n735:                                            (:response entry))\n737:                                     [(:response entry) kept]\n738:                                     (:response entry) kept)}\n794:(defn- portable-response?\n795:  \"True when install `response` survives the body's own codec.\"\n796:  [jing-codec? response]\n798:    (try (jing.cbor/encode response) true\n800:    (cbor/portable-value? response)))\n805:   response that spawned it, preserving the child's phase and waits\n808:   response outside the canonical bytes domain refuses it too.  Under\n815:          (map (fn [[m {:keys [vm phase parent response]}]]\n816:                 (when-not (portable-response? jing-codec? response)\n817:                   (non-portable! :install-response {:yin.k/name m}))\n829:                       :yin.k/response response\n985:   joining the export's own.  Extra AST metadata never travels; an AST\n1109:   explicit park: its continuation is the response wrapper.\"\n1144:   :call #{:next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request :link-response\n1320:  \"The module declarations the completion walk asks for: each linked\n1379:   arbitration, counter and the exclusive policy, and the origin when\n1387:          (assoc (select-keys header [:yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration\n1413:   arbitration, next-op-seq, origin when the task has a predecessor, and\n1486:                                                     [:response :manifest\n1614:                             :yin.k/requires\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '248,283p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '300,336p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '1020,1098p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1,95p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; sed -n '900,945p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '2040,2075p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; rg -n 'byte|string|decimal|ratio|metadata|numeric' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '248,283p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '300,336p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '1020,1098p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1,95p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; sed -n '900,945p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '2040,2075p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; rg -n 'byte|string|decimal|ratio|metadata|numeric' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md\"","aggregated_output":"\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The found map and the canonical order of a version-1 export\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- new-found\n  []\n  {:cells {} :cell-of {} :markers {} :stores #{} :segments #{}})\n\n\n(defn- ordered\n  \"`coll` in the order the export walks it: by the canonical bytes of\n   `(keyfn x)`'s encoding under a version-1 export, so cell numbering\n   never depends on a host's map or set iteration; as given under\n   version 0, whose wire is frozen.\"\n  [found keyfn coll]\n  (if-some [order (:order @found)]\n    (order keyfn coll)\n    coll))\n\n\n(declare value-encoder registers-v2)\n\n\n(defn- canonical-order\n  \"The ordering function of a version-1 export: `(order keyfn coll)`\n   sorts `coll` by the canonical bytes of the scratch encoding of each\n   `(keyfn x)`.  The scratch encoder owns a found map of its own, so it\n   mints nothing the export keeps; it runs under `:ordering`, so every\n   cell its keys meet is seeded by the identity the cell table keys by,\n   never by a number a comparison could shift, and it orders its own\n   nested maps the same way.  Ties keep the walk order given: wait\n   order and alias identity are never reordered.\"\n  [vm serve! v2]\n  (let [self (volatile! nil)\n        order (fn [keyfn coll]\n  \"A host function as UCF 7.5.2's primitive marker: its one name in\n   `vm`'s primitives, with that name's profile.  Unnamed, ambiguous\n   and host-state functions are not portable.\"\n  [vm f]\n  (let [n (vm/name-of (:primitives vm)\n                      (or (:primitive-canonical-names vm) {}) f)\n        profile (when (symbol? n)\n                  (vm/profile-of (:primitives vm) n))]\n    (cond\n      (nil? n) (non-portable! :unnamed-function {})\n      (not (symbol? n)) (non-portable! :ambiguous-primitive {})\n      (= :host (:yin.k/class profile))\n      (non-portable! :host-state-primitive {:yin.k/name n})\n      :else {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/primitive\n             :yin.k/name n\n             :yin.k/profile (:yin.k/profile profile)})))\n\n\n(defn- value-encoder\n  \"The encode half, over `vm`'s coordinates with the export's ONE\n   found map: closures lift through the kernel (recording their origin\n   segment and module store), a stream or cursor reference is\n   authenticated under the source's own secret before it is encoded --\n   a forged reference refuses with its kind -- and every literal map is\n   wrapped, so a program's data can never forge a marker.  A host\n   object, a reified continuation and a heap cell refuse (S7.5.4).\"\n  [vm serve! found]\n  (letfn [(encode\n            [x]\n            (cond\n              (scalar? x) x\n              ;; the canonical codec's numeric carriers (Float64, Decimal,\n              ;; Rational and the like, none of them `number?` on every\n              ;; host) are version-1 content: the jing body carries them\n              ;; as-is, and the stream codec's frozen version-0 domain --\n              ;; `portable-value?` -- does not admit them\n              (jing.cbor/numeric? x)\n  `:yin.k/request-args` are optional restatements of the envelope.\n- `:link-request` and `:link-response`: `:yin.k/link-id`,\n  `:yin.k/name`, `:yin.k/request`, `:yin.k/response`, `:yin.k/cell`;\n  `:link-request` also `:yin.k/envelope`.\n- `:install`: `:yin.k/name`, and the install entry above.\n\n## §7.5 Recursive portable encoding (blocker 2)\n\n### 7.5.1 A disjoint tagged grammar\n\nEncoding is one total function `encode : value × path → outcome`, applied\nrecursively to every value reachable from the frame: accumulator, operand\nstack, environment values, every frame in K, parked records, and the store\nslice of §7.6.2. The outcome is either the portable form or a refusal naming\nthe path.\n\nThe first draft encoded markers as ordinary maps — `{:yin.k/primitive '+}`,\n`{:yin.k/ref addr}` — while passing program map literals through in their\nnatural shape. That is ambiguous to everyone: §2.5 admits map literals,\nso a program can *contain* `{:yin.k/primitive '+}`, and a decoder cannot tell\nan encoder's marker from a program's data. The grammar here is disjoint by\nconstruction: **every map at an encoded-value position is a UCF marker**, and\na program's own maps are always wrapped.\n\n| Encoded form | Tag | Contents | Decodes to |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| scalars: nil, boolean, number, string, keyword, symbol | — | themselves (never a map; cannot forge a marker) | themselves |\n| vector, list, set | — | same shape, elements recursively encoded (never a map) | same shape, elements decoded |\n| literal map | `:yin.k/literal` | `:yin.k/entries [k₁ v₁ k₂ v₂ …]`, keys and values recursively encoded | the map, keys and values decoded |\n| closure | `:yin.k/closure` | `:yin.k/segment addr :yin.k/entry pc :yin.k/params […] :yin.k/env {encoded}` | `{:type :closure …}` (§2.4) |\n| reified continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame (§7.4.1), recursively | `{:type :reified-continuation …}` |\n| parked continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame plus `:yin.k/parked-id` | `{:type :parked-continuation …}` |\n| primitive | `:yin.k/primitive` | `:yin.k/name sym` | the named function, after profile check (§7.5.2) |\n| stream reference | `:yin.k/stream` | `:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …` | an attached handle, under a fresh store key |\n| cursor reference | `:yin.k/cursor-ref` | `:yin.k/cell cell-id` | a store cursor entry for that cell, §7.5.3 |\n| shared subvalue | `:yin.k/ref` | `:yin.k/id addr` | the table entry at `addr`, §7.5.3 |\n| anything else | — | — | **refused**: `:yin.k/non-portable` |\n\nWhy the wrapping is total rather than an \"escaping rule\" for suspicious\nshapes: a literal map is wrapped *wherever it occurs and whatever it\ncontains* — including one that happens to read `{:yin.k/tag …}` — and the\nentries inside a wrapper are themselves encoded, so a forged marker inside a\nliteral is re-wrapped one level down and peeled back exactly one level on\ndecode. No rule asks whether a map \"looks like\" data; no decoder decision\ndepends on program content. Scalar and collection values pass through only\nbecause no marker is ever a non-map, which the closed tag table fixes.\n\nThe scalar arm's \"number\" includes every exact-integer carrier Jing\nsupports, not only what a host's `number?` admits: a JS or Dart `BigInt`\nis a scalar like a `long`, encoded as itself and carried by Jing's major\ntypes 0/1 and tags 2/3 (C3 converged ruling 4). No marker is added, and a\nbig integer inside a heap cell stays under the cell-lift refusal. Likewise\nJing's float64 carrier, how an integral float keeps its kind on JavaScript,\nis a scalar number (float-address mob ruling); `yin.vm/plain-data?` and\n`machine-data?` admit it for rows and machine payloads too, and rows,\nimages and hashing keep its bytes. Admission and round-trip preservation\nof a numeric scalar do not imply that every primitive accepts it: on\nJavaScript the standard arithmetic and ordering primitives meet the\ncarrier's coercion refusal (`dao.jing.cbor.md`), while the JVM and Dart\ncompute on their native double. That error is Jing's `:carrier-coercion`,\nnot a lift refusal (section 7.9). Only a profile with an explicit seam\ncomputes on floats portably.\n\nMap keys are values and are encoded as such — inside `:yin.k/entries`\nalternately with their values, and nowhere else; the frame's own `:yin.k/env`\nkeys are the program's symbols and are structural, not encoded values.\n\n**Decoding validates, independent of hashing.** A hash check confirms what\nwas encoded; it does not confirm the encoding is well formed. The decoder\nwalks the grammar and rejects, as `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the path: a map\nwith no `:yin.k/tag` at an encoded-value position (impossible from a\nconforming encoder); a tag outside the closed set; a marker missing its\nrequired keys; a `:yin.k/ref` whose address is absent from the value table; a\nref graph that is cyclic *through ref data* (content-addressed table entries\ncannot mint such a cycle — a cycle can only be written by hand, and it is\nrejected); and a value table entry not referenced by anything reachable.\nMalformed-but-correctly-hashed input fails here, before any lowering.\n\n**Closure markers carry a binding discipline and a store (linker M4).**\n# UCF version 2: four execution profiles\n\nStatus: normative architectural amendment, D10b-A; implementation is\nD10b-B, not claimed here. Author: gpt-6-astra. Source baseline: 9cea60dd.\nDate: 2026-10-07.\n\nThis is an append-only amendment to\n[yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md](yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md)\n(UCF), especially 7.2.1, 7.3, 7.4, 7.5, 7.6, 7.7.4 and 7.11.1.\nIt implements the D10b grammar ruling of 2026-10-06. It is authoritative\nfor body version 2 where it differs from the parent document. The parent\nremains authoritative for versions 0 and 1. A statement about an existing\nfunction below fixes its behavior at the named source baseline, not at an\narbitrary future revision of that function.\n\n## 1. Scope and compatibility\n\nVersion 2 transports the native suspended state of semantic, de Bruijn\nstack, de Bruijn register and AST-walker tasks. It does not translate a\ncontinuation between kernels. Every profile works on JVM, JavaScript/Node\nand Dart. Cross-host portability is required within a profile; equality\nof bytes between different profiles is neither required nor expected.\n\nVersions 0 and 1 retain their grammar, reader rules and bytes. Their\nexport selection must not silently switch to version 2. The already\napproved D14 repair that includes omitted transitive module stores in\nversion-1 exports is part of the baseline: its corrected addresses stay\ncorrected. This amendment neither reverses that repair nor permits any\nadditional legacy-byte change. Existing checkpoint and ledger pins stay\nunchanged.\n\nThere is no change to any instruction, AST-row, scalar-class, liveness,\nmodule-manifest, DaoStream-outcome or lease vocabulary. In particular,\nregister code remains r2. No boundary opcode, operand, live-slot index,\nregister allocation or opcode arity is added. The new contract versions\ncontinuation data and its interpretation, not executable code.\n\nThe four profiles are the following exact closed combinations:\n\n| `:yin.k/engine` | `:yin.code/contract` | inner `:yin.k/version` | Kernel |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `:semantic` | `\"v3\"` | `1` | `yin.vm.semantic` |\n| `:stack` | `\"b2\"` | `1` | `yin.vm.debruijn.stack` |\n| `:register` | `\"r2\"` | `1` | `yin.vm.debruijn.register` |\n| `:walker` | `\"v3\"` | `1` | `yin.vm.ast-walker` |\n\nThe entire profile map, with exactly these three keys, travels in\n`:yin.k/contract`. The body itself has `:yin.k/version 2`. The old\n`ucf/contract-stamp` is not changed; a separate version-2 profile registry\nowns these combinations. Engine identifiers above are literal unqualified\nkeywords. They are not the kernel's runtime `:format` keyword.\n\nA receiver declares supported profiles through composition. An unsupported\ncombination, including a missing/malformed profile declaration, is\n`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. No inference from image shape, receiver type,\ncode contents or custody policy may replace the declaration. One task\nand its install tree use one identical profile. A supported but different\nprofile on a child is a structural `:yin.k/undecodable` error after the\nwhole-tree profile gate. Mixed-kernel installs require a future contract;\nall four homogeneous trees are required now.\n\n## 2. Notation and closed structural grammar\n\n`V` means the encoded guest-value grammar in section 6, not an arbitrary\nhost value. `N` means an exact CBOR integer in `[0, 2^52-1]`, never a\nfloat64 carrier which happens to have an integral value. This bound\napplies to counters, PCs, offsets, lengths, register indices and layout\nlength sums. Smaller kernel-specific limits also apply. Signed guest\nintegers and guest numeric values are not bounded by this notation.\n\n`A` is a code address in the enclosing profile's native address domain\n(section 4). `B` is a Jing segment address of canonical body bytes.\n`M` is a module-store manifest address. `P` is a parked identifier in the\nexisting engine identifier domain. `C` is a task-local cell identifier.\n`S` is a symbol. A vector is ordered. A map is unordered and has unique\nkeys by canonical-byte equality. `?` in a table means a key may be absent;\nit does not mean a present nil is accepted. Empty maps/vectors are emitted\nexplicitly for required fields. All structural maps in this amendment\nare closed: unlisted keys refuse. Optional absence has one spelling.\nGuest literal maps remain open data because they are wrapped as values.\n\nAll named integer/version checks distinguish wire scalar kinds before\nhost numeric equality. Canonical bytes are Jing's canonical CBOR bytes;\nretain float64 and other numeric carrier classes while validating hashes.\nOnly the established execution bridge converts scalars for execution.\nDo not recompute a code hash after coercing `1.0` to `1` on JavaScript.\n\nAn environment `E` has this exact shape:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/bindings {S V ...}\n :yin.k/store-of M}                 ; optional; omit outside a module\n```\n\nLower reconstructs the native named environment, adding\nrequest the link again, which is a second effect the parked task\nnever made. That is the **name-only install sketch**, and it must\nnever be exported as task state. The complete shape is the pending\ntogether with the body's install entry for the same name:\n\n```clojure\n;; the waiter's pending: unchanged, never sufficient alone\n{:yin.k/reason :install\n :yin.k/name   'foo}\n\n;; the body's entry for that name (7.2.1)\n:yin.k/installs\n{'foo {:yin.k/phase    :running   ; the child's phase (linker 7.3)\n       :yin.k/parent   [:t0 7]    ; the link id that spawned it\n       :yin.k/response {...}      ; the verified link response\n       :yin.k/child    {...}}}    ; the child's whole handoff body\n```\n\n- The response is what a foreign resumer spawns its own fresh child\n  from, and what the child's exports are validated against at halt.\n  It is carried verbatim and must be in the canonical bytes domain,\n  else lift refuses `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:install-response`.\n- A response is verified by the resumer, not trusted because it\n  decodes. Its manifest names the module the entry is keyed by, its\n  link id equals `:yin.k/parent`, and its image and manifest pass the\n  checks the linker applied at delivery (`yin.vm.linker.md` sections\n  5 and 9): the image is well formed, hashes to the address it is\n  delivered under, and is the image the manifest names. A failure is\n  `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the entry's response.\n- A live install entry carries phase `:running` or `:parked`. Its\n  child independently satisfies the handoff grammar and the\n  quiescence requirements, with kind `:blocked`, `:parked`, or\n  `:halted`. The phase records scheduler progress; it does not\n  substitute for validation of the child's actual machine state,\n  and no phase requires or forbids a kind: a `:running` phase with\n  a blocked child is valid. A runnable, non-quiescent child refuses\n  export. Lower preserves both fields, and the scheduler continues\n  from the restored child without rerunning initialization. Other\n  install phases are not exportable live-child entries, and any\n  other phase value is `:yin.k/undecodable`. No stricter relation\n  between phase and kind is introduced until a scheduler\n  normalization invariant is specified and proved; M-next D tests\n  every combination of the two phases and three kinds, explicit\n  park included.\n- The resumer restores the child's carried state into the fresh\n  child before the scheduler may step it. The fresh child is a\nsection publishes the grammar that 7.7.5 argues for. The reasons stay\nin 7.7.5; the runtime, ledger, and step contract stay in\n`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.2. Nothing here adds a key to the lease\nvocabulary or to a DaoStream outcome map. Every rule applies to\nversion-1 bodies only (7.2.1).\n\n**Exact integers.** `:yin.k/epoch`, `:yin.k/seq` and\n`:yin.k/next-op-seq` are nonnegative exact integers no greater than\n2^52-1 (4503599627370495), the bound within which every host compares\nand increments exactly (`dao.lease.md`, *Keys*, uses the same one).\nThe kind is the canonical codec's integer kind: a float is not an\ninteger even when it is integral. A value outside the range or of\nanother kind makes its carrier defective: a body is\n`:yin.k/undecodable`, an envelope or a binding establishes nothing.\nNo counter wraps, saturates silently, or restarts.\n\n**Operation sequence state.** A task under a grant holds one counter,\n`:yin.k/next-op-seq`, on its root. Install children draw from it.\n\n1. *Assign.* Before the first attempt of a write to an enrolled\n   stream, the fenced writer takes `n`, the counter's value, forms\n   the id `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` with `P` the\n   occurrence its grant names, stores the id on the wait entry, and\n   sets the counter to `n+1`. The three happen as one step over the\n   machine value, before any append.\n2. *Retain.* The id stays on the entry while the write is unfinished:\n   a `full` path, an append of unknown effect, an acceptance with no\n   admission outcome yet, or a `:suspended` outcome. A retry reuses\n   the id and never takes another.\n3. *Carry.* At a park the id travels in the pending (7.4.3) and the\n   counter in the header (7.2.1). The successor's counter is the\n   holder's counter at that park, so the sequence continues along\n   the chain and never restarts at a new occurrence.\n4. *Restore.* Lower sets the fresh task's counter to the carried\n   value exactly. There is no local value to take a maximum with:\n   the receiving task is new (contrast `:yin.k/id-counter`, 7.6.3).\n22:of bytes between different profiles is neither required nor expected.\n24:Versions 0 and 1 retain their grammar, reader rules and bytes. Their\n29:additional legacy-byte change. Existing checkpoint and ledger pins stay\n54:combination, including a missing/malformed profile declaration, is\n56:code contents or custody policy may replace the declaration. One task\n69:integers and guest numeric values are not bounded by this notation.\n72:(section 4). `B` is a Jing segment address of canonical body bytes.\n76:keys by canonical-byte equality. `?` in a table means a key may be absent;\n83:host numeric equality. Canonical bytes are Jing's canonical CBOR bytes;\n84:retain float64 and other numeric carrier classes while validating hashes.\n162:correlation IDs and operation-ID rules are unchanged. The complete\n172:`{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/origin\n178:| Root blocked/parked, exclusive first | policy, occurrence, arbitration, next-op-seq | new custody subject |\n188:strings. Arbitration is exactly the existing identity/descriptor pair.\n192:operation scope and baseline equality remain UCF 7.7.8 rules.\n194:A fork tree forbids carried custody operation IDs. An exclusive tree\n195:checks every retained operation, including descendants, against the\n201:checkpoint address, protection declaration, authenticated binding and\n202:replay-prefix evidence, lease/holder, arbitration transaction identity,\n228:Stack H and register R are their existing hash strings, not new\n231:`encode-register-image`. Preserve the current string/hex framing exactly.\n232:A profile declaration is not prefixed to these inputs: their existing\n234:remain Jing hashes of whole canonical body bytes, distinct from A.\n251:`vm/semantic-bytecode-grammar`. Check row arity, slot kinds, Rule R,\n458:Extra AST metadata never travels. Source-position metadata is not state.\n555:and complete restoration-grammar validator before it answers OK. A valid\n626:IDs and request bytes are unchanged; no retry mints a new ID.\n637:1. Decode the canonical codec while retaining numeric classes. Check\n647:   before hashing code, attachment, proposal or restoration.\n649:   canonical bytes and the requested body address, native code hashes,\n654:4. For an exclusive root, run the existing occurrence/baseline/operation\n675:baseline is inspectable but cannot allocate another operation; export\n692:operation IDs and the root's exact next-op-seq; D11 assigns only genuinely\n693:new operations. Replay starts from the existing zero-to-frontier prefix\n704:Restoration itself delivers no wait result. A test's controlled later\n710:## 10. Export recovery, integration and implementation boundary\n716:not an opaque Closure codec. All recovery/body binding, metadata,\n717:descriptor, alias and byte-reproduction checks remain mandatory.\n719:Rehydration leaves root and children exporting with waits outside runnable\n721:execution. A recovery object's enrolled declaration is preparation data,\n738:the required authority/holder version-dispatch integration and tests.\n788:   counter restoration and child gates. Exclusive halt has no grant or\n793:10. **Canonical bytes.** Use identical prepared data in each profile on\n794:    all hosts, varying only map insertion order; assert exact body bytes,\n800:    rehydrate a version-2 recovery snapshot; assert exact published bytes,\n802:    restarted abort. Reject recovery bytes as a handoff body.\n805:    modules/resources only; assert identical behavior and declarations.\n817:- The exact profile strings come from `vm/*-contract`, not informal\n819:  string v3 but differ by engine; neither is inferred from that string.\n834:  legacy stamp, format or address migration is part of this amendment.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"The census fixes the missing cell-content commitment, and the tagged value union separates literals from references. The numbering claim still fails: equal cell bodies do not make a tie symmetric when map values differ or another binding identifies one of the cells. I’m checking the remaining canonicalization and lowering rules against the amended grammar.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '155,168p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; sed -n '217,264p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; sed -n '542,594p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; sed -n '2370,2425p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '2740,2837p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '2050,2085p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '1,65p' docs/design/datom.world.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '155,168p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; sed -n '217,264p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; sed -n '542,594p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md; sed -n '2370,2425p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '2740,2837p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '2050,2085p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; sed -n '1,65p' docs/design/datom.world.md\"","aggregated_output":"rule, including an explicitly parked child. Every install pending names\nan entry here. Response manifest, image format, contract and child\nprofile must agree. No child initialization runs during lower.\n\nPending is the closed union already specified by UCF 7.4.3:\n`:next`, `:put`, `:ffi`, `:ffi-request`, `:link-request`, `:link-response`,\n`:install`. Their field shapes, stream/cell references, envelopes,\ncorrelation IDs and operation-ID rules are unchanged. The complete\n`:install` form, not the old name-only sketch, is required. Version 2\nadds no observation, cursor-mint or close pending arm. Encoding and\nvalidation must dispatch a pending before walking its V-valued fields;\na stream descriptor or a code row is not a guest literal map.\n\n## 3. Custody arms, roles and admission\n## 4. Code identity and image layout\n\nThese are the address functions, with no UCF wrapper hash substituted:\n\n| Profile | `:yin.k/code` value | Key computation |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| semantic | canonical instruction vector | `ucf/code-address`, i.e. `jing/segment-key vector` |\n| stack | unrelocated admitted instruction vector | `dcode/image-hash vector` |\n| register | unrelocated `{:bodies [...] :instructions [...]}` | `rcode/register-hash image` |\n| walker | `[id tag & slots]` | `id = jing/segment-key [tag & slots]`, map key equals id |\n\nStack H and register R are their existing hash strings, not new\n`:segment/...` wrappers. H hashes the existing descriptor hash followed\nby `encode-image`; R hashes its descriptor followed by\n`encode-register-image`. Preserve the current string/hex framing exactly.\nA profile declaration is not prefixed to these inputs: their existing\ndescriptors/code contracts already determine their domains. Body addresses\nremain Jing hashes of whole canonical body bytes, distinct from A.\n\nCode data slots use their existing raw code-scalar grammar, not V. A\nmap literal in an instruction or AST row is code data; never dispatch it\nas a continuation/resource marker. Conversely, authentic runtime values\ninside saved frames use V and cannot be hidden in a code constant to\nescape ownership or dependency checks.\n\nValidate each image with its own published validator. A semantic image\nuses `code/well-formed-vector?`; stack uses `dcode/image-defect`; register\nuses `rcode/register-image-defect`. The empty stack vector and empty\nregister image `{:bodies [] :instructions []}` are admitted only as the\nempty base code space, as their loaders already allow. Do not validate a\nconcatenation as if it were a newly compiled single image.\n\nWalker rows use the complete v3 table in\n[code-as-tuples](yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md), section 2, implemented by\n`vm/semantic-bytecode-grammar`. Check row arity, slot kinds, Rule R,\nidentity and all node/nodes references, not just the root hash. Row maps\nare flattened addressed storage, never serialized map ASTs. Every child\nreference must resolve locally; reference cycles are rejected. A module's\nroot is its image identity; it is not a hash of the rows map. There may\nbe multiple roots contributed by closures and continuation frames.\n\nFor semantic/walker, requires.segments equals the keys of code and code\nis precisely the dependency closure. For stack/register it includes all\nimages of the current layout and any independently needed module image;\nall such images must be addressed. Do not prune the middle of a layout\nbecause its instructions appear dead: that changes absolute PCs.\n\n### 4.1 Ordered layouts\n## 7. Eligibility and safepoint validation\n\n### 7.1 Whole-task requirements\n\nOnly blocked, explicitly parked or halted tasks at the existing engine\nexport boundary are eligible. Running CESK/PC snapshots are not a fourth\nkind. All ready queues are empty, all installs complete in the UCF sense,\nand root/child state is stable. Refuse any root or child with an observe\nwait, unapplied held observation, reachable unminted cursor, pending close,\nor link request without its installed response cursor. These are D4-D9\nholds, not occasions to invent a new pending variant or opcode.\n\nA successful lift is checked by the version-aware checkpoint inspector\nand complete restoration-grammar validator before it answers OK. A valid\ncode image alone proves neither a valid saved continuation nor a valid\ntask safepoint. Safepoint tables remain derived data, not stored flags.\n\n### 7.2 PC profiles\n\nFor semantic, derive safepoints with the existing ucf/safepoints semantics.\nFor stack, the site is resume PC minus one, in the captured layout; the\nsame opcode-to-reason table below applies. For register, the carried\nsite-pc and PC satisfy section 5.3 and the existing r2 validator.\n\n| Site opcode | Allowed pending reasons |\n| --- | --- |\n| `:stream-next` | next |\n| `:stream-put` | put |\n| `:ffi-call` | ffi, ffi-request |\n| `:call` | next, put, ffi, ffi-request, link-request, link-response, install |\n| `:park` | none; explicit parked record only |\n| `:current-continuation` | none; captured-value record only |\n\nReason names in this table are the corresponding unqualified keywords.\nCall permits dynamic effects; the pending must independently prove the\nactual effect and all correlation/resource constraints. Do not infer an\nFFI request merely from a call opcode. Parked-table entries in PC profiles\nmust name explicit park sites. FFI bookkeeping parked records are handled\nby 7.3, not mislabeled as explicit parks. A captured-value R must name a\ncurrent-continuation site. Return-frame sites are non-tail calls.\n\nFor stack, the saved resume PC and every return PC are strictly inside\nthe captured code-space length. An admitted b2 image ends at a jump,\nreturn or halt, not a suspending instruction; no valid park needs an\nout-of-range resume fetch. The general row-at one-past rule does not\nwaive this eligibility check. Register's inclusive body ranges and\nvalidator govern its PC endpoints. Semantic retains its existing strict\nPC bound. All lexical/stack and\nregister invariants of section 5 remain mandatory in addition to the\ntable. No blanket assumption that every positive PC is a boundary.\n\n### 7.3 Walker and FFI normalization\n\n(defn- check-frame-pc-v2\n  \"The wire reasons the resume site admits, per profile.\"\n  [engine {:keys [code]} pending registers path]\n  (case engine\n    :semantic (check-frame-pc code pending registers path)\n    :walker\n    (let [top (:yin.k/k registers)\n          t (:yin.k/type top)\n          reason (:yin.k/reason pending)\n          bad! (fn []\n                 (undecodable! {:yin.k/kind :ffi-correlation\n                                :yin.k/reason reason\n                                :yin.k/path path}))]\n      (case reason\n        :ffi (when-not (and (= :dao.stream.apply/eval-call t)\n                            (= (:yin.k/call-id pending) (:yin.k/call-id top)))\n               (bad!))\n        :ffi-request (when-not (and (= :dao.stream.apply/request-sent t)\n                                    (= (:yin.k/call-id pending)\n                                       (:yin.k/parked-id top))\n                                    (= (:yin.k/request-op pending)\n                                       (:yin.k/op top)))\n                       (bad!))\n        (when (contains? #{:dao.stream.apply/eval-call\n                           :dao.stream.apply/request-sent}\n                         t)\n          (bad!))))\n    (:stack :register)\n    (let [composed (compose-v2 engine (mapv code (:yin.k/layout registers)))\n          op (site-op engine {:segment (:segment composed)\n                              :pc (:yin.k/pc registers)\n                              :site-pc (:yin.k/site-pc registers)})]\n      (when-not (contains? (get site-reasons op) (:yin.k/reason pending))\n        (undecodable! {:yin.k/kind :reason-mismatch\n                       :yin.k/pc (:yin.k/pc registers)\n                       :yin.k/reason (:yin.k/reason pending)\n                       :yin.k/path path})))\n    (undecodable! {:yin.k/kind :unsupported-profile :yin.k/path path})))\n\n\n(defn- guarded-hash\n  \"`(f image)`, or nil when the image is so malformed that hashing it\n   throws: a code entry that cannot be hashed is not an image.\"\n  [f image]\n  (try (f image)\n       (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _ nil)))\n\n\n(defn- validate-code-v2\n  \"Every code entry hashes to its own key and is admissible under the\n   profile's own validator.\"\n  [engine code]\n  (doseq [[a v] code]\n    (when (and (contains? #{:stack :register} engine)\n               (nil? (guarded-hash (if (= :stack engine)\n                                     dcode/image-hash\n                       :yin.k/kind :blocked-without-frames}))\n      (let [ctx {:code code\n                 :layout (:yin.k/layout body)\n                 :parked (:yin.k/parked body)}]\n        (doseq [[i frame] (map-indexed vector frames)]\n          (let [path [:yin.k/frames i]\n                pending (:yin.k/pending frame)\n                reason (:yin.k/reason pending)]\n            (closed! frame #{:yin.k/registers :yin.k/pending} #{} path)\n            (when-not (and (map? pending) (contains? pending-keys reason))\n              (undecodable! {:yin.k/path (conj path :yin.k/pending)\n                             :yin.k/reason reason}))\n            (let [[required optional] (get pending-keys reason)]\n              (closed! pending required optional (conj path :yin.k/pending)))\n            (validate-registers-v2 engine ctx (:yin.k/registers frame)\n                                   (conj path :yin.k/registers) :wait)\n            (validate-pending cells pending (conj path :yin.k/pending))\n            (check-frame-pc-v2 engine ctx pending (:yin.k/registers frame)\n                               (conj path :yin.k/registers))))\n        (when (= :parked kind)\n          (when-not (contains? (:yin.k/parked body) (:yin.k/parked-id body))\n            (undecodable! {:yin.k/parked-id (:yin.k/parked-id body)\n                           :yin.k/path [:yin.k/parked-id]})))\n        (doseq [[pid r] (:yin.k/parked body)]\n          (let [path [:yin.k/parked pid]]\n            (validate-registers-v2 engine ctx r path :parked)\n            (parked-site! engine ctx pid r path)))\n        (when (and (= :halted kind) (seq frames))\n          (undecodable! {:yin.k/path [:yin.k/frames]}))\n        (let [roots (reachable-values body)]\n          (validate-closures-v2 engine code roots)\n          (doseq [root roots\n                  f (tagged-of :yin.k/frame root)]\n            (let [path [:yin.k/frame]]\n              (if (contains? f :yin.k/parked-id)\n                (do (closed! f #{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/parked-id} #{} path)\n                    (when-not (contains? (:yin.k/parked body)\n                                         (:yin.k/parked-id f))\n                      (undecodable! {:yin.k/parked-id (:yin.k/parked-id f)\n                                     :yin.k/path path})))\n                (do (closed! f #{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/registers} #{} path)\n                    (validate-registers-v2 engine ctx (:yin.k/registers f)\n                                           (conj path :yin.k/registers)\n                                           :captured)))))))\n      (doseq [[m inst] (:yin.k/installs body)]\n        (validate-install-v2 m inst)\n        (validate-body-v2 (:yin.k/child inst))\n        (when-not (= (:yin.k/contract body)\n                     (:yin.k/contract (:yin.k/child inst)))\n          (undecodable! {:yin.k/name m :yin.k/kind :mixed-profile\n                         :yin.k/path [:yin.k/installs m :yin.k/child\n                                      :yin.k/contract]})))\n      (doseq [[i frame] (map-indexed vector frames)]\n        (let [pending (:yin.k/pending frame)]\n          (when (and (= :install (:yin.k/reason pending))\n                     (not (contains? (:yin.k/installs body)\n                                     (:yin.k/name pending))))\n            (undecodable! {:yin.k/name (:yin.k/name pending)\n                           :yin.k/kind :incomplete-install\n                           :yin.k/path [:yin.k/frames i :yin.k/pending]}))))\n      (when (= :walker engine)\n        (walker-bookkeeping! body))\n      (let [named (if (= :walker engine)\n                    (let [roots (walker-roots body)\n                          closure (walker-closure! code roots)]\n                      (validate-walker-rows! code roots)\n                      closure)\n                    (into (referenced-segments-v2 body)\n                          (:yin.k/layout body)))\n            carried (set (keys code))]\n        (when-some [a (first (sort-by str (set/difference named carried)))]\n          (undecodable! {:yin.k/segment a :yin.k/path [:yin.k/code]}))\n        (when-some [a (first (sort-by str (set/difference carried named)))]\n          (undecodable! {:yin.k/segment a :yin.k/kind :extra-code\n                         :yin.k/path [:yin.k/code a]}))\n        (when-not (= carried (:yin.k/segments (:yin.k/requires body)))\n          (undecodable! {:yin.k/path [:yin.k/requires :yin.k/segments]})))\n      (let [stores (set (keys (:yin.k/module-stores body)))\n            roots (reachable-values body)\n            wanted (into #{}\n                         (comp (mapcat #(tree-seq coll?\n                                                  (fn [n]\n                                                    (if (map? n)\n                                                      (concat (keys n) (vals n))\n                                                      (seq n)))\n                                                  %))\n                               (filter map?)\n                               (keep :yin.k/store-of))\n                         (conj roots (:yin.k/free-env body)))]\n        (when-some [m (first (sort-by str (set/difference wanted stores)))]\n          (non-portable! :missing-module-store {engine/store-of-key m})))\n      (let [referenced (referenced-cells body)\n            missing (remove #(contains? cells %) referenced)\n            extra (remove #(contains? referenced %) (keys cells))]\n        (when-some [cid (first missing)]\n          (undecodable! {:yin.k/cell cid :yin.k/path [:yin.k/cells]}))\n        (when-some [cid (first (sort-by str extra))]\n          (undecodable! {:yin.k/cell cid :yin.k/kind :extra-cell\n  (doseq [k (keys (:yin.k/bindings e))]\n    (when-not (symbol? k)\n      (undecodable! {:yin.k/path (conj path :yin.k/bindings)\n                     :yin.k/kind :binding-name}))))\n\n\n(defn- instruction-at\n  [v pc]\n  (when (and (exact-int? pc) (< -1 pc (count v)))\n    (nth v pc)))\n\n\n(def ^:private context-sites\n  \"The resume-site mnemonics each register context admits: a wait one\n   of the four effect sites, an explicit park its `:park`, a captured\n   continuation its `:current-continuation`.\"\n  {:wait #{:call :stream-put :stream-next :ffi-call}\n   :parked #{:park}\n   :captured #{:current-continuation}})\n\n\n(defn- layout-prefix!\n  \"`l` is a nonempty prefix of the task layout, every image carried.\"\n  [{:keys [code layout]} l path]\n  (when-not (and (vector? l) (seq l)\n                 (<= (count l) (count layout))\n                 (= l (subvec layout 0 (count l)))\n                 (every? #(contains? code %) l))\n    (undecodable! {:yin.k/path (conj path :yin.k/layout)\n                   :yin.k/kind :layout-not-prefix})))\n\n\n(defn- stack-payload-v2\n  \"The native-shaped skeleton of one stack register map, its code space\n   rebuilt from its own layout; the wire values stay as they are, since\n   only counts and shapes are judged.\"\n---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-09 17:27:42 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-10 00:27:42 Asia/Bangkok\n\nReviewed `e0c843c976a5f1177563194a11a9b97b943e698f`. The requested diff reports one changed file, 529 insertions and 327 deletions. Re-read the revised document in full and checked the governing amendments and relevant source. No files edited; no tests run.\n\nThe census and value union resolve the two principal round-1 defects. Canonical numbering still fails, and validation has remaining gaps that prevent approving this as the published design to build against.\n\n1. **HIGH — architectural defect | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:343 | Residual ties are not necessarily symmetric.** Consider an environment with `'a` bound to a set containing cells X and Y, and `'b` bound to X. X and Y have identical stream/position bodies. Traversal visits `'a` first. Choosing X first produces `'b → [:cell c-1]`; choosing Y first produces `'b → [:cell c-2]`. The census is identical, but the root differs. Likewise, map keys X and Y with values 0 and 1 have identical key skeletons and cell-body tie-breaks, yet exchanging their encounter order changes the encoded map. | Replace encounter-order residual ties with canonicalization of the **whole alias topology**. A valid reference algorithm can enumerate cell renamings, canonically encode the complete task under each, and select the least encoding. Optimization can follow later. Local body equality is insufficient; map values and references elsewhere must participate. Specify recursive skeleton construction, including nested unordered containers and treatment of context edges.\n\n2. **HIGH — specification gap affecting equality and round trip | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:399 | Canonical numbering is not complete canonical-form validation.** The steps do not expressly validate row ordering, unique binding/map/store keys, unique module/install/parked identifiers, or decoded map/set collisions. These collections are row vectors, so Jing’s map/set checks do not enforce their semantics. A correctly hashed `:bindings` row can contain one symbol twice; lowering to a map loses an entry. The schema also needs to reject alternative spellings such as a reference-free collection row where `[:lit d]` is required. | Add canonical representation checks before restoration: ordering, uniqueness under canonical bytes **and portable decoded equality**, exact optional-field spelling, and the literal-versus-row rule. Require canonical empty structures and admissible `[:row B]` target kinds explicitly. Otherwise different valid encodings can lower to the same state, and some lose information.\n\n3. **HIGH — specification gap affecting safe lowering | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:419 | Several governing semantic checks remain unstated.** Captured `:reified` registers must identify a `:current-continuation` site; the draft checks wait and parked sites but omits this context. An install response must be independently verified against its name, parent id, manifest, image address, format, contract, and child profile—“verified response” in the row description does not specify receiver verification. Merely checking that correlation fields are present also omits stream/cell and envelope/correlation agreement. Step 8 evaluates declared requirements without explicitly checking their agreement with the actual dependency census. | Incorporate these checks from amendment §§2, 6–8 and UCF §7.4.3. Require the code map and segment requirements to match the governing complete closure. Check census usage through actual task roots, excluding the census’s own declaration edges: otherwise an unused census entry is trivially “reachable.”\n\n4. **HIGH — contract conflict | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:306 | The halted custody rule excludes ordinary fork results.** The text says a halted root carries origin only. Amendment §3 distinguishes a fork halt with no custody header from an exclusive halt with origin only. A first fork halt has no origin to carry. | Specify both halted arms: fork halt has no custody data; exclusive halt has origin only. Also document the projection from the new explicit fork-policy slot to the amendment’s empty-header fork representation. Preserve the governing admission semantics rather than describing all custody shapes as unchanged.\n\n5. **HIGH — value-domain conflict | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:149 | The literal domain narrows existing numeric support.** “Exact integers … float64 carriers” omits decimals and ratios. UCF §7.5.1 admits numbers, amendment §6 retains Jing scalar classes, and `value-encoder` explicitly admits Jing Decimal and Rational carriers. This conflicts with the claims that value semantics and profiles are untouched and that legacy bodies normalize with semantic preservation. | Include the existing admitted numeric classes and their Jing normalization rules. Separately define the portable **structural-data** domain: install responses are canonical-byte-domain data under UCF §7.4.3, not necessarily guest literals. Their current `response-V` slot must not accidentally reject otherwise valid response data.\n\n6. **MEDIUM — specification gap | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:156 | Metadata remains outside the recursive reference decision.** Preserving whatever Jing preserves does not establish that metadata contains only literal data. Metadata can contain an authentic cursor reference even when a collection’s elements are literal. Copying that reference as ordinary metadata retains source coordinates/seals rather than remapping it. | Traverse retained metadata when deciding portability and whether a value reaches references. With no metadata slot on collection rows, explicitly refuse reference-bearing metadata by path. Preserve reference-free metadata under Jing’s normalization rules; distinguish stripped reader metadata from retained metadata.\n\n7. **MEDIUM — implementation specification gap | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:391 | The wire entry point and supported-profile boundary remain unspecified.** The root version can be gated only after locating a root row, but the pack/header/root-locator grammar is absent. Step 1 also traverses an unvalidated install graph, so malformed cycles or duplicate/conflicting row ids must not make gating loop or select arbitrary data. Section 2 says the carrier admits profiles whose closed row tables do not yet exist. | Define a minimal framing/root-locator contract and a bounded, cycle-safe preliminary gate traversal. Distinguish malformed task structure from unsupported version/profile. Until other tables are published, declare only semantic version-3 support; other profiles must fail closed at the profile gate. Hash verification should dispatch on the address’s registered algorithm, not an unspecified default.\n\n8. **MEDIUM — specification gap | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:464 | The law domains are improved but still too broad.** A valid canonical state in a supported profile may lack required resources or custody evidence, so `lower(S)` need not produce a machine. “Compared as carried, not after max” also requires a specified reconstruction mechanism; ordinary relift observes the restored counter. Legacy correspondence currently promises normalization for every legacy body despite newly declared refusal cases. | Restrict canonical round trip to successful lowering with adequate composition/admission inputs and no intervening execution. Specify how the reconstruction context preserves carried canonical fields. Make legacy normalization partial with explicit refusals, or prove coverage of every previously admitted body. Exact inversion should remain limited to the canonical nested projection.\n\nTwo editorial corrections remain: `:noncanonical-cells` is described as a lower-side `:undecodable` kind at line 355 but a new lift-side kind at line 447; line 420 should state reason mismatch under `:yin.k/undecodable`, rather than appearing to introduce another outcome family.\n\nAnswers to §10.7–10.9:\n\n7. **Other profile tables:** Their deferral is acceptable for a semantic-only Draft. Require the complete stack/register/walker tables and their validation rules before declaring those profiles supported by version 3. Their current profiles and code addresses must remain unchanged.\n\n8. **Legacy readers versus normalization:** Recommend direct version-aware lowering of versions 0–2, with normalization as a separate explicit tool. Reading an old checkpoint should preserve its address and custody binding. A normalized version-3 checkpoint has a different address and must not silently replace an admitted legacy checkpoint.\n\n9. **Skeleton placeholder:** `[:cell nil]` cannot collide with `[:lit [:cell nil]]` **if the full value-union tag is retained in the skeleton**. Confirm that conditional property, not the current numbering algorithm. Skeleton expansion must preserve row-kind/value distinctions and terminate without expanding cyclic context lookups. The placeholder does not solve the alias-topology counterexample in finding 1.\n\nProperties that passed:\n\n- **Census:** The root now commits to each declared cell’s stream and position. Changing either changes the cell-body, census, and root address, subject to the ordinary digest assumption. I found no additional carried payload omitted from that commitment. Semantic equality up to cell renaming remains blocked by canonicalization.\n- **Value union:** Exact two-element `[:lit d]`, `[:row B]`, and `[:cell C]` forms are unambiguous. Literal address keywords and literal vectors resembling references remain data. Metadata traversal and target-kind validation are separate remaining obligations.\n- **Task completeness:** Ordered waits, parked records, module stores, install entries/children, code, and operation-sequence state now have explicit places. Registers are correctly separated from waits.\n- **Pending variants:** The seven dynamic reasons and retained envelopes/op-ids replace the superseded park/call-effect sketches.\n- **Versioning:** Body version 3 avoids redefining version 2; unchanged code contracts and inner profile version are appropriate.\n- **Module context:** Environment bindings and `store-of` are now separate and preserved.\n- **Graph discipline:** Structural references and context lookups are distinguished; module self-reference and parked-reference cycles need not become cyclic guest values.\n- **Invariants:** The approach requires no hidden global state, callbacks, implicit control flow, or shared mutable native state. Transport/query interpretation remains separate from native execution. Explicit references and validation preserve the intended graph discipline once the listed gaps are closed.\n- **Laws:** Native observational equivalence and controlled-identity cross-engine comparison have the correct direction and purpose. Legacy normalization is correctly distinguished from exact historical-byte inversion.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED — fix findings 1–5 before publication; fill 6–8 and reconcile diagnostics before the implementation brief. Merge would publish a Draft, not accept the design."}}
